::SPEECH_U02_L06_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L06_QZ_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Point: Note cards help speakers stay organized. Which support best fits?
{
~A. Note cards are small.#This is a detail but does not explain organization.
=B. A note card can list each main point in the order the speaker will present it.#Correct. This support explains how note cards help organization.
~C. Some note cards are different colors.#This does not support the point clearly.
~D. Speeches can happen in many rooms.#This is unrelated to note cards.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L06_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L06_QZ_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which transition best connects rehearsal to delivery?
{
~A. Next thing.#This is vague and does not show a relationship.
~B. Whatever comes after that.#This is informal and unclear because it does not show a relationship.
~C. I am moving on now.#This announces movement but does not explain the connection.
=D. After rehearsal helps control timing, delivery practice helps the speaker sound natural.#Correct. This shows how the ideas connect.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L06_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L06_QZ_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which sentence is reasoning?
{
=A. This example matters because timing helps the audience follow the speech without feeling rushed.#Correct. It explains why the support matters.
~B. Timing exists in speeches.#This is a statement but not reasoning.
~C. The next point is notes.#This is a transition, not reasoning.
~D. Practice is a word.#This does not explain support.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L06_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L06_QZ_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which note is most speech-ready?
{
~A. Say stuff about preparation.#This is too vague because it does not identify a specific point or support cue.
~B. Read a full paragraph aloud.#This may prevent natural delivery because it encourages reading instead of presenting.
~C. Maybe talk more.#This does not guide the speaker.
=D. Point 1: rehearsal improves timing; support: time each section.#Correct. This gives a clear point and support cue.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L06_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L06_QZ_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which support is too general?
{
=A. Practice helps.#Correct. This does not explain how practice helps.
~B. Timing each section shows whether a point is too long.#This is specific support.
~C. A note card can list the order of ideas.#This specifically supports organization.
~D. A chart can show a pattern visually.#This is specific support.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L06_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L06_QZ_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
What should a transition do?
{
~A. Hide the next idea.#Transitions should guide, not hide.
=B. Show how one idea connects to the next.#Correct. A purposeful transition explains the relationship between ideas.
~C. Replace all support.#Support is still needed.
~D. Add unrelated information.#Transitions should connect related ideas.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L06_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L06_QZ_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which set follows the strongest development pattern?
{
~A. Transition, random fact, unrelated opinion.#This does not develop an informative point.
~B. Opinion, joke, no explanation.#This does not fit informative development because it lacks clear support and reasoning.
~C. Conclusion, topic change, missing support.#This is not clear development.
=D. Point, support, reasoning, transition.#Correct. This pattern develops and connects the idea.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L06_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L06_QZ_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which transition signals sequence?
{
~A. However, note cards also have limits.#However signals contrast, so it does not show step-by-step order.
~B. Because practice builds comfort, speakers may feel ready.#Because signals cause, so it does not show sequence.
=C. First, identify the point you need to support.#Correct. First signals sequence.
~D. In contrast, silent practice differs from peer practice.#In contrast signals comparison.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L06_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L06_QZ_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which support best fits the point "Visual aids can clarify information"?
{
=A. A chart can show changes over time so the audience can see the pattern.#Correct. This gives a specific visual aid and explains how it clarifies.
~B. Visual aids are things.#This is vague because it does not explain how the visual aid helps the audience.
~C. Some people like colors.#This does not clearly support the point.
~D. Speeches can be short.#This is unrelated to visual aids and does not explain how they clarify information.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L06_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L06_QZ_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which note best follows quality expectations?
{
~A. Support: good thing.#This is too vague for quality work.
~B. Transition: next.#This is weak because it does not show a relationship.
=C. Point 2: visual aid shows pattern; reasoning: audience can see change quickly.#Correct. This note is clear and useful.
~D. Say all the words.#This does not support speech-ready format.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L06_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L06_QZ_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which sentence repeats instead of reasons?
{
~A. This matters because the audience can follow the order more easily.#This gives reasoning because it explains why the support helps listeners.
=B. The support is useful because it is useful.#Correct. This repeats the idea and does not explain why the support matters.
~C. This example shows how a timer helps the speaker adjust length.#This explains the support instead of merely repeating it.
~D. This detail helps listeners see the pattern.#This gives reasoning because it explains how the detail helps listeners.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L06_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L06_QZ_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which transition signals cause and effect?
{
~A. However, another option is peer practice.#However signals contrast.
~B. First, choose a topic.#First signals sequence, so it does not show a cause-and-effect relationship.
=C. As a result, the speaker may feel more prepared.#Correct. As a result signals an effect.
~D. Similarly, both strategies require practice.#Similarly signals comparison.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L06_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L06_QZ_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which support best fits "organized notes improve delivery"?
{
=A. Notes grouped by main point help a speaker find the next idea quickly.#Correct. This specific support explains how notes improve delivery.
~B. Notes can be written with pencils.#This detail does not explain delivery.
~C. Some speakers like blue paper.#This is unrelated to delivery quality.
~D. Delivery is part of speaking.#This repeats the topic without support.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L06_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L06_QZ_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Why should support be included in a speech outline?
{
~A. To make the outline confusing.#Support should clarify ideas.
=B. To show how each point will be explained to the audience.#Correct. Support makes the speech product more complete and useful.
~C. To replace the focus.#The focus is still needed.
~D. To add unrelated material.#Support should connect to the point.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L06_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L06_QZ_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which transition best signals contrast?
{
~A. First, write the focus.#First signals sequence, so it does not show contrast between ideas.
~B. Because practice helps timing.#Because signals cause, so it does not show contrast.
=C. However, timing practice does not replace organized notes.#Correct. However signals contrast.
~D. Therefore, the speaker is prepared.#Therefore signals result.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L06_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L06_QZ_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which answer best improves weak support "visual aids are helpful"?
{
~A. Visual aids are helpful because they are helpful.#This repeats the point instead of adding stronger support.
~B. Visual aids exist.#This is too general because it does not explain how the visual aid helps the audience.
~C. Visual aids are not connected to speeches.#This contradicts the point.
=D. A simple chart can show a pattern the audience might miss in spoken words alone.#Correct. This gives specific support and explanation.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L06_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L06_QZ_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which choice best supports audience understanding?
{
=A. Specific support followed by reasoning.#Correct. The audience needs both detail and explanation.
~B. Unsupported main points.#Unsupported points leave the audience with questions.
~C. Transitions with no relationship.#Transitions should show relationships.
~D. Random examples.#Random examples weaken focus.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L06_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L06_QZ_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which outline section is most complete?
{
~A. Point: practice.#This is too vague because it does not state a complete informative point.
~B. Point: transition.#This does not develop an informative idea.
~C. Point: stuff about notes.#This is vague and incomplete because it gives no clear speaking cue.
=D. Point: rehearsal improves timing; support: time each section; reasoning: revise parts that run long.#Correct. It includes point, support, and reasoning.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L06_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L06_QZ_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
What makes support relevant?
{
~A. It is unrelated but interesting.#Unrelated support weakens focus.
~B. It is the longest sentence.#Length does not prove relevance.
=C. It directly explains or proves the speech point.#Correct. Relevant support connects to the point.
~D. It avoids the topic.#Support should stay on topic.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L06_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L06_QZ_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which transition best moves from notes to rehearsal?
{
~A. Notes exist.#This does not transition.
~B. I am done with notes.#This does not show a relationship.
~C. Randomly, rehearsal.#This is unclear because it does not show how notes connect to rehearsal.
=D. Once notes are organized, rehearsal helps the speaker practice using them.#Correct. This transition connects notes and rehearsal.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L06_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L06_QZ_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which support should be removed from a point about rehearsal improving timing?
{
=A. Some cafeteria lines are long.#Correct. This is unrelated to rehearsal and timing.
~B. A timer can show whether the speech is too long.#This directly supports timing.
~C. Practicing aloud can reveal sections that need shortening.#This supports rehearsal and timing.
~D. Rehearsal can help the speaker adjust pacing.#This supports the point.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L06_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L06_QZ_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which speech note is too vague?
{
~A. Point 1: planner shows deadlines.#This is clear and specific, so it is not the vague note.
=B. Talk about all the things.#Correct. This gives no clear point or support.
~C. Evidence: note card lists main points.#This gives a specific cue.
~D. Transition: after notes, practice delivery.#This gives a relationship cue.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L06_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L06_QZ_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Why are transitions important for listeners?
{
~A. They make the speech harder to follow.#Transitions should make the speech easier to follow.
~B. They replace all support.#Support is still needed.
=C. They show how ideas are connected.#Correct. Transitions guide listeners between points.
~D. They remove the need for organization.#Transitions are part of organization.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L06_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L06_QZ_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which phrase best introduces reasoning?
{
~A. Randomly speaking...#This does not signal reasoning.
=B. This matters because...#Correct. This phrase prepares the speaker to explain why support matters.
~C. On another topic...#This signals a topic shift.
~D. Forget that point...#This does not explain support.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L06_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L06_QZ_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which statement best summarizes this lesson?
{
=A. Informative speech points need specific support, reasoning, and transitions that guide the audience.#Correct. This summarizes the lesson focus.
~B. Informative speech points should avoid support.#Support is necessary for clear explanation.
~C. Transitions should be random.#Transitions should show relationships.
~D. Reasoning should repeat the point only.#Reasoning should explain why support matters.
}
